\section{A real small-sample caveat, confirmed empirically -- read before using}{

Cross-validated AUC computed by pooling out-of-fold predictions (the only
option for leave-one-out, where each fold has one held-out point) is
NOT centered on 0.5 under a true null for small, weakly- or non-
predictive cohorts -- it is biased downward, and the bias gets WORSE as
the method gets closer to leave-one-out. Confirmed here with an
independent from-scratch reference implementation (not just this
function): at n=40 with a single pure-noise predictor, mean AUC across
60 null draws was 0.33 for LOO, 0.40 for 10-fold, and 0.45 for 3-fold --
all below the naively-expected 0.5, and this is a property of small-
sample cross-validated AUC generally, not a bug specific to this
implementation. \strong{Do not compare an observed AUC to 0.5 as the null
expectation for a small cohort.} Use \code{n_permutations > 0} (below) to
get the actual null distribution FOR YOUR data's sample size and CV
settings, and report the resulting permutation p-value instead of (or
alongside) the raw AUC.
